WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:07.339
Beginning as a standalone film in 1994, Stargate
spawned a slew of TV series and spin-off movies.

00:00:07.339 --> 00:00:12.500
Since the franchise spans decades, there have
been quite a few cast changes over the years.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.160
We're here to let you in on the real reasons
why these actors left the Stargate franchise.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.430
In the original film, we all remember how
Kurt Russell played Colonel Jack O'Neill,

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:25.420
while James Spader played Dr. Daniel Jackson.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.420
"Forty-five seconds."

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.420
"He's leaving.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.850
Turn it off."

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:32.290
"I'm trying to, I can't disarm it."

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:37.179
Though the iconic duo were perfect for their
roles, neither of them went on to act in the

00:00:37.179 --> 00:00:42.379
spin-off series SG-1 -- where O'Neill is played
by MacGyver vet Richard Dean Anderson, and

00:00:42.379 --> 00:00:44.739
Jackson is played by Michael Shanks.

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:49.640
What could have caused Russell and Spader
to jump ship after the original film's success?

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.430
There isn't one definitive answer, but we
can sum it up by saying it just wasn't in

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:54.430
the cards for them.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:58.469
The original filmmakers had nothing to do
with SG-1, so the series creators ended up

00:00:58.469 --> 00:01:03.010
tweaking various aspects of the universe to
better suit a sprawling series.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:09.159
For example, the enemy alien Ra was supposedly
part of an extinct alien species in the movie,

00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:11.880
but the TV series completely contradicted
that.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.920
And besides this, it took three years after
the release of the film for the series to

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.259
hit the airwaves.

00:01:17.259 --> 00:01:21.250
By this time, Russell and Spader had moved
on to other projects -- none of them in the

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.890
realm of television.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:27.130
Unlike today, moving from film to TV in the
nineties was seen as a demotion.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.570
Possibly the biggest name on the show, Richard
Dean Anderson took the role of Colonel Jack

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:37.460
O'Neill a few years after his long stint as
problem solver extraordinaire MacGyver in

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.109
the late eighties and early nineties.

00:01:39.109 --> 00:01:43.939
Due to this, he brought an endless parade
of fans with him when he signed on to the

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:45.590
series in 1997.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:49.999
He played the Colonel diligently for many
years, but stepped down at the end of season

00:01:49.999 --> 00:01:53.960
eight -- only appearing in select episodes
of seasons nine and ten.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:58.319
Subsequently, the writers were forced to change
the trajectory of the show and introduce a

00:01:58.319 --> 00:02:03.740
replacement leader for the SG-1 team, which
would ultimately be the character of Cameron

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.180
Mitchell -- played by Ben Browder.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.050
According to an interview with GateWorld,
he said he quit the show in order to spend

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.660
more time with his daughter.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:17.220
His only child, Wylie Quinn Anderson, was
born in 1998, during the second season of

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.220
SG-1.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.980
As the show progressed, he felt more and more
guilty for forfeiting family time for the

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.860
many demands of his role.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:28.040
By seasons six and seven, the producers had
started to reduce his workload.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.700
By season eight, he had become a minor character
altogether, and only appeared occasionally

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:33.960
from that point forward.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.940
Amanda Tapping played the highly intelligent
Samantha Carter, who begins her arc as a military

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:41.950
cadet before eventually getting promoted.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:46.330
“We don't know sir, but it branched out
from very specific areas.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:49.890
Network and language software, followed by
system and application software."

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:55.110
She was a member of the cast for all ten seasons,
and then reprised her role in the fourth season

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:58.180
of the spin-off series Stargate: Atlantis.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:01.540
After that, she only showed up once in a while
as a guest actor.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:03.260
So where did she go?

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:07.440
According to an interview with SheKnows, she
left the franchise to focus on her own show

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.890
for the Syfy Network, which was called Sanctuary.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:15.480
In addition to starring in the series, she
also produced and directed a few episodes.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:20.290
After all, she had experience to call upon
from her SG-1 days, having directed the episode

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.340
"Resurrection".

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.510
It's pretty likely that these forays into
behind-the-camera work helped her develop

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:26.740
higher ambitions.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:32.930
Michael Shanks played archeologist Dr. Daniel
Jackson, who sort of served as the conscience

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:35.010
and cultural liaison of the SG-1.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:36.650
"Nem, you have your answer.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:38.350
Now let us go."

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:43.010
Though he ultimately remained for the entirety
of the show, he did temporarily leave during

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.510
the sixth season.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:48.790
He didn't get terribly specific about the
reasons for his departure, but he has said

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:51.430
that his character wasn't being used to his
full potential.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.850
Shanks said in an interview with Cyberex:

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:58.510
"I had begun to think the show would work
just as well without me, and that's when I

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:00.380
felt that I had to go."

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:04.600
This is understandable, since at the end of
season five, Daniel Jackson had finished his

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:09.130
story arc with his wife, Sha'uri -- which
was the whole reason his character even joined

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:10.930
SG-1 to begin with.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:16.280
At the end of season five, Jackson "ascended",
which is sort of like dying -- but not really.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.590
The writers brought him back in season seven
by having him "descend" back into the mortal

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:21.590
realm.

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:24.970
Altogether, Shanks didn't have any qualms
with the writing team, and later insisted:

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:29.540
"I thought the writing staff was just great
and did a marvelous job."

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.760
Corin Nemec initially played a minor character
named Jonas Quinn, who was introduced in season

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:35.760
five.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.490
In the following season, Quinn was revamped
as Daniel Jackson's replacement -- since Michael

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:40.800
Shanks had left the show.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.490
"Let's give it a shot."

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:48.380
An optimistic alien capable of learning at
incredible speeds, Quinn was eager to prove

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:53.290
himself valuable to his people -- the alien
race known as the Langarians.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:58.310
Even more so, he wanted to prove himself to
the SG-1, and was deeply afraid of failure.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:03.540
Though most fans were likely miffed by the
sudden departure of Dr. Jackson, Quinn quickly

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:05.570
found his way into everyone's hearts.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:10.210
This success was short-lived, however, since
Jackson made his return in season seven.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:14.600
The result forced Jonas Quinn to become a
minor character again, and he only appeared

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.290
as a guest a handful of times after that.

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:21.320
Nemec was surely a gentleman about his short
tenure, and was just happy to have been on

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:22.320
the show at all.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.580
In an interview with Kurt Manwaring, he said:

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:28.500
"Michael [Shanks] originated with that show,
his return was very welcome.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.740
Though of course disappointed, I was still
happy for Michael.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:36.280
Being an actor on a great show like Stargate
is the best life ever."

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.970
Don S. Davis played the leader of Stargate
Command, General George Hammond.

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:45.290
His character managed the multiple teams that
traveled through the Stargate, including the

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:46.290
SG-1.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:50.600
Despite his elite status, he was nonetheless
a benevolent leader who didn't let bureaucrats

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.180
sabotage the program.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:56.350
He fully supported his personnel and believed
in never leaving soldiers behind.

00:05:56.350 --> 00:06:01.180
Davis played Hammond for eight straight seasons
before health problems forced him to reexamine

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:02.180
his priorities.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:05.700
So, for seasons nine and ten, he was only
a guest actor.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:09.670
In season nine, he was promoted and thus left
Stargate command.

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:13.340
Hank Landry - played by Beau Bridges - stepped
in to replace him.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:17.140
Fans hated to see him go, but it was in no
way a hard thing to understand.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:18.810
Health always takes precedence.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.990
Davis reprised his role in Stargate: Continuum.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:27.370
However, this was sadly his last appearance,
as he passed away from a heart attack in 2008.

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:31.380
In the Stargate universe, the writers gave
him the same fate - in which he passed away

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.790
off-screen.

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:37.900
To commemorate him, the franchise named a
spaceship after him -- the U.S.S. Hammond.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:42.340
Rainbow Sun Francks played Lieutenant Aiden
Ford -- one of the main characters of the

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.520
spinoff series Stargate: Atlantis.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:48.160
He was an expert in explosives and a guy who
was willing to put his life on the line to

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.480
save his teammates, which he demonstrates
on a few occasions.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:53.690
"Hey man, this isn't supposed to be fun."

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:57.810
But Ford would be the first major loss of
Stargate: Atlantis.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:03.470
He was replaced after one season by Ronon
Dex, played by Jason Momoa, and became a minor

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:05.169
character in season two.

00:07:05.169 --> 00:07:09.240
The reasons for this are not crystal clear,
but it has been speculated that the writers

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.900
simply wanted to mix things up and keep things
interesting.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.139
Ford just happened to be the first victim.

00:07:14.139 --> 00:07:15.949
He explained in an interview with GateWorld:

00:07:15.949 --> 00:07:21.040
"I think [...] they want to make sure that
a hit show has lots of twists and lots of

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:22.330
this and that.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:25.240
Something had to happen, and I just happened
to be the one."

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:29.169
In his eyes, his character didn't get the
development that he had hoped for in season

00:07:29.169 --> 00:07:33.090
one, and perhaps another season would've given
his character the time to prove himself as

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:34.090
a mainstay.

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:36.770
Regardless, he holds no grudges.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:42.490
Torri Higginson, who played Dr. Elizabeth
Weir, is another casualty of Stargate: Atlantis.

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:47.360
During the transition from season three to
season four, Stargate SG-1 had finally ended

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:52.699
its ten-season run -- which meant it would
no longer be a lead-in to Stargate: Atlantis.

00:07:52.699 --> 00:07:56.360
Because of this, the writers again felt the
need to freshen things up by shifting characters

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:57.360
around.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.330
Weir was the leader of the Atlantis expedition,
and along the way, she proved to be an exceptional

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:04.070
manager and negotiator.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:06.410
"You must understand our situation.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:08.790
If you can't stop Oberoth..."

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:09.840
"I said I would do everything in my power."

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:15.210
Sadly, in season four, Weir sustained severe
injuries and could no longer lead the Atlantis

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:16.210
team.

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:19.850
This was the show's way of relegating her
to a minor character, and she eventually faded

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:21.040
from the show altogether.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:26.470
Another unfortunate casualty of Stargate:
Atlantis was Dr. Carson Beckett -- played

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:28.199
by Paul McGillion.

00:08:28.199 --> 00:08:32.630
He was the chief medical officer, and one
of the more compassionate and thoughtful characters.

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:33.729
"It was definitely human."

00:08:33.729 --> 00:08:36.740
"I don't know, maybe it was a nosebleed."

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:40.079
"Is that supposed to be a joke, Colonel?"

00:08:40.079 --> 00:08:44.279
Beckett was a fan favorite, and he played
his part well for multiple seasons.

00:08:44.279 --> 00:08:48.689
Much to everyone's dismay, though, he died
unexpectedly in an explosion near the end

00:08:48.689 --> 00:08:50.110
of season three.

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:55.420
He did sort of return in season four and five,
but only as a clone and in a much more minor

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:56.420
role.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:09:00.230
It was McGillion's belief that this sudden
end is yet another example of the writers

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:04.310
and producers feeling the need to shock the
audience and always keep them on the edge

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:05.310
of their seats.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:09.920
It's sad, but at least, in a roundabout way,
they complimented McGillion.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:14.449
They wanted to kill off a beloved character
-- for shock value -- and that's exactly what

00:09:14.449 --> 00:09:15.449
they did.

00:09:15.449 --> 00:09:17.980
The effect rippled throughout the show's fanbase.

00:09:19.069 --> 00:09:23.930
Teryl Rothery was technically a guest star,
but nonetheless felt like a main character.

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:27.050
It probably helped that she appeared in over
70 episodes.

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:32.860
Rothery played Dr. Janet Fraiser, the gentle
physician who healed SG-1 of the wounds they

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:33.860
suffered off-world.

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:36.600
"The MRI was negative.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:40.819
The DNA screen matches what we have on file."

00:09:40.819 --> 00:09:45.630
While Fraiser usually remained at Stargate
Command on Earth, she would sometimes leave

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:50.490
the base and work in the field as a medic
for soldiers who were wounded during firefights.

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:55.050
As a result, she was occasionally in harm's
way, and in season seven of SG-1, she was

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:56.610
shot and killed.

00:09:56.610 --> 00:10:01.230
It was quite a shock for Stargate fans -- who
had been warned via a teaser trailer that

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:03.800
an important character was going to die.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:08.170
Though it was hinted that it might be Jack
O'Neill, it turned out to be Fraiser.

00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:12.880
Rothery got to show up one last time on the
season nine episode "Ripple Effect", where

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:16.009
she appeared as a version of Fraiser from
a parallel universe.

00:10:16.009 --> 00:10:20.829
It was reported that the show's executives
pressured writers to kill off a character.

00:10:20.829 --> 00:10:25.459
Co-star Don S. Davis was frustrated by the
decision to kill off Frasier, and called the

00:10:25.459 --> 00:10:26.740
higher-up responsible:

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:29.110
"Some stupid bean counter."

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:33.379
According to Davis, the decision was made
because the show had moved from Showtime to

00:10:33.379 --> 00:10:38.350
the Syfy Channel, and the new network wanted
something major to happen to hook viewers.

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:42.829
Ultimately, that something turned out to be
Fraiser's untimely death.

